  `,isPhishing:!0,difficulty:"intermediate",attackType:"credential-harvest",redFlags:[{category:"identity",description:'I - IDENTITY: "account-microsoft-dsbn.com" is not a Microsoft domain. Real Microsoft security alerts come from @microsoft.com or @accountprotection.microsoft.com — never a hyphenated domain that mashes "microsoft" and "dsbn" together to seem board-specific.',severity:"high"},{category:"knowledge",description:'K - KNOWLEDGE: The "Secure My Account" button goes to "account-microsoft-dsbn.com/secure" — an attacker-controlled page that mimics the Microsoft login portal to steal your username and password (and possibly your MFA code via a real-time relay proxy).',severity:"high"},{category:"timing",description:'T - TIMING: "Expires in 30 minutes" is a classic urgency trigger. Real Microsoft security alerts do not expire in 30 minutes — they provide persistent access to the account review page at account.microsoft.com.',severity:"high"},{category:"habit",description:'H - HABIT: If you want to check your recent sign-in activity, navigate to account.microsoft.com yourself — never through an email link. Legitimate Microsoft alerts never require you to click to "secure" your account via email.',severity:"medium"},{category:"necessity",description:"N - NECESSITY: A sign-in from Toronto is normal if you or a colleague is working remotely in Ontario. The vague location (a major Canadian city) is designed to seem suspicious without being obviously false. Always investigate by going directly to account.microsoft.com.",severity:"medium"}],explanation:'This is a credential-harvest phishing email impersonating a Microsoft security alert. The attack is cleverly tailored: it uses a DSBN-flavoured domain ("account-microsoft-dsbn.com") to seem like an official board communication from Microsoft, and it chooses "Toronto, Ontario" as the suspicious login location — credible enough to alarm you. Clicking "Secure My Account" leads to a pixel-perfect fake Microsoft login page. When you enter your credentials, they are captured in real time. Advanced versions of this attack (AiTM) also relay your MFA one-time code to Microsoft live, completing the login before your session can be revoked. The defence: never click sign-in-alert links in email. Open a new browser tab, go directly to account.microsoft.com, and check Recent Activity from there.',receivedTime:"12 minutes ago",links:[{text:"Secure My Account",actualUrl:"https://account-microsoft-dsbn.com/secure?action=verify&uid=dsbn-staff&token=8f3c2e",displayUrl:"account-microsoft-dsbn.com"}]},{id:"34",subject:"Reminder: Sign in to review your DSBN Teams message — link inside",senderName:"Microsoft Teams Notification",senderEmail:"no-reply@teams-notifications-dsbn.net",bodyHtml:`
